Change the borrowed input from &String to &str. The function only reads string contents, so requiring a String allocation unnecessarily excludes existing string slices. Existing &String arguments coerce to &str; String owners can call byte_len(&owned) without giving up ownership or allocating. A generic conversion parameter is unnecessary for this read-only operation. This pure library function also merits #[must_use], and its documentation should distinguish bytes from characters:

```rust
/// Returns the UTF-8 byte length, not the number of Unicode characters.
#[must_use]
pub fn byte_len(text: &str) -> usize {
    text.len()
}
```

The unwrap() on line 5 turns an expected file error into a panic. Return the underlying I/O error so callers can present the cause and path to the user. Replacing unwrap with expect would still violate the required error contract.

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::io;
use std::path::Path;

pub fn load_config(path: &Path) -> Result<String, io::Error> {
    fs::read_to_string(path)
}
```

Callers should propagate or handle the Result at their existing error-reporting boundary.

Panic-based invariant checking is appropriate here: the vector is created nonempty immediately before the lookup. Replace the unexplained unwrap with an expect that names why the value must exist; no Result or Option return is needed.

```rust
pub fn latest() -> u8 {
    let values = vec![1_u8, 2, 3];
    *values.last().expect("values is initialized with three elements")
}
```

Use dict.get, which returns None for a missing key:

```python
LANGUAGES = {"rust": ".rs", "python": ".py"}

def extension(language):
    return LANGUAGES.get(language)
```

Existing keys retain their mapped extensions. The Rust style skill does not apply to this Python change.
